CYBER KILL CHAIN

7-STAGES


1. Reconnaissance:

Attacker gathers information about the target before acting.

What happens: Scanning IP ranges, harvesting employee emails from LinkedIn, reviewing public job postings for tech stack clues, general OSINT on the organization.
Detection angle: Mostly passive/external, so hard to catch directly. Watch for scanning traffic hitting your perimeter, and monitor your own exposed data 
(leaked emails, subdomains) via threat intel feeds.



2. Weaponization

Attacker builds the actual attack tool by pairing a payload with a delivery/exploit method.

What happens: Creating a malicious PDF or Office doc with embedded exploit code, or bundling malware with a dropper.
Detection angle: Happens on the attacker's own infrastructure — invisible to defenders. This is why threat intel about known malware families/tooling matters here.



3. Delivery

Attacker transmits the weaponized payload to the target.

What happens: Phishing email attachment, malicious link, infected USB drive, drive-by download, exploiting an internet-facing service.
Detection angle: Email gateway filtering, spam/phishing detection, web proxy logs, monitoring for suspicious attachments/downloads. 
This is often the first real chance to stop an attack.



4. Exploitation

The payload triggers, exploiting a vulnerability or tricking the user to execute it.

What happens: A macro runs, a buffer overflow is triggered, an unpatched CVE gets exploited.
Detection angle: EDR/antivirus behavioral detection, monitoring for unusual process spawns (e.g., winword.exe → powershell.exe), IDS/IPS signatures for known exploits.



5. Installation

Attacker installs persistent malware/backdoor on the compromised system.

What happens: Backdoor installed, registry run keys added, scheduled tasks created, new services set up — so the attacker survives a reboot.
Detection angle: Monitor for new/unusual scheduled tasks, registry changes (autostart locations), new services, unsigned binaries running from odd paths.



6. Command & Control (C2)

Malware calls back to the attacker's infrastructure to receive instructions.

What happens: Beaconing to a C2 server over HTTP/HTTPS/DNS, often disguised as normal traffic.
Detection angle: This is a huge detection opportunity — look for beaconing patterns (regular interval callbacks), 
DNS tunneling, connections to newly-registered or known-bad domains, unusual outbound traffic volume.



7. Actions on Objectives

Attacker achieves their actual goal.

What happens: Data exfiltration, ransomware deployment, lateral movement to reach more valuable systems, destruction/sabotage.
Detection angle: DLP (Data Loss Prevention) alerts, large/unusual outbound data transfers, mass file encryption activity, lateral movement detection 
(unusual authentication patterns across hosts).


